    "q-16557": {
      "dom_id": "step-q16557",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14169858/assets/aa0b48e9042aafdd70db2d008c826abc6dc04bcb916504860117b87cd86c7fbe.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(3,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
